{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5d7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a16f29-8a52-78e0-a3b7-ac73629a118d"},"sequence":326,"recorded_at":1780531400000647,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a16f29-9139-7d63-bf27-9f131bb65f9f","payload_type":"run.terminal.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_terminal","command_id":"01a16f29-9139-7d63-bf27-9f131bb65f9f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a16f29-9139-7d63-bf27-9f131bb65f9f"},"terminal":"completed","text":"PANEL-NONCE: bbe2cd25194ea9b3\nARTIFACT-SHA256: d736aad93aceb28a508c81975ab089eb3920aff6f4711a52bcbdcc0d63b19e7e\nКанон `~/Мой диск/Context/AGENTS.md` прочитан целиком до `<!-- КАНОН-КОНЕЦ -->`; проектных файлов правил в рабочей папке нет.\n\nVisionary review (k=3): The candidate is architecturally aligned with `architecture-packet.json` and strengthens, rather than widens, the approved design. AD-001 is implemented at the single decode choke point per bridge: `_loads_lenient`/`_recover_trailing_braces(strict,quiet)` in `bridges/opencode-zen-bridge/server.py` and the intentionally duplicated `_loads_tolerant`/`_recover_trailing_braces` in `core/tool_emulation.py` and `bridges/muse-bridge/server.py`. Recovery is cold-path only after `json.loads` (and `strict=False` only in opencode-zen) fails, requires `isinstance(obj,dict)`, `1<=extra<=16`, and a tail of only whitespace/`}` via `tail.strip(\" \\t\\r\\n}\")`, and normalises `RecursionError` to `ValueError`. This narrow grammar is the right long-term answer to formatting drift: it absorbs the observed over-closing without inventing brace-balance repair, trailing-comma or text tolerance that could silently execute a differently-intended call (RISK-001/ASM-001). Parity respects BND-002: core/muse keep strict control-character decoding (`JSONDecoder()`), no cross-bridge imports, no `ToolCallParser`/`strict_live` changes; the only asymmetry — opencode-zen `_normalize_arguments` now also recovers a string-encoded arguments object via `_loads_lenient` — is disclosed in `coder.json`, additive, and confined to the already-lenient bridge.\n\nAD-002/BND-001: `_find_close_in` is correctly extracted from `ToolCallParser._find_close_outside_string` (string/escape-aware, no semantic change) and reused by `_strip_broken_tool_blocks`. The three sinks — `emit_parsed_content` wrapper in `Handler._stream_sse`, unconditional strip after `_split_tool_calls` in `Handler._serve_json`, and `_incomplete_tool_step_prompt` — preserve `_split_tool_calls`, `_is_incomplete_tool_step` and retry caps, while `_BROKEN_BLOCK_NOTICE` only when no tool calls and no visible text avoids empty/502 replies (CD-001 backward compatible). The fail-closed 17th-candidate tail drop (`tool_strip_cap_hit max=16`) deliberately supersedes the packet's original fail-open wording in AD-002/RISK-002; from a resilience standpoint this is correct evolution — QA-001's zero-leaked-tags budget outweighs preserving a pathological tail, and CPU stays bounded at O(16·n) despite `_code_context` being O(n) per candidate (VER-004: 0.42/0.148/1.949 ms < 5 ms). Accepted trade-off, disclosed: a replayed history with >16 separately-closed blocks loses the tail in `_sanitize_assistant_text` (sinks never see valid blocks), and the Google-direct stream path remains out of scope; neither blocks this incident fix, both should be tracked if those surfaces grow.\n\nAD-003: `_sanitize_assistant_text` uses `quiet=True` (threaded through `_loads_lenient`/`_parse_tool_call_block(s)`) preventing `tool_call_recovered` log amplification on history replay, and returns the original object byte-identical when `dropped==0`, preserving valid single/batch and quoted/fenced blocks. The OpenAI unwrap `if \"name\" not in obj and isinstance(obj.get(\"function\"),dict)` in all three parsers correctly makes top-level `name` win and still rejects present-but-blank/non-string names — a durable guard against models echoing their own history envelope.\n\nAD-004/CD-001: keeping three standalone stdlib copies with parity enforced by `tests/test_tool_parser.py:TestCrossBridgeParserContract` plus `TestTrailingBraceRecovery` (tails `}`,`}}}`,`} }\\n}`, 16 accepted/17 rejected, garbage/comma/text/under-closed/`[1]}` rejected, 100k-deep `RecursionError` contained, batch-segment recovery, function-wrapper/name-wins) is the right long-term structure for single-file bridge deployment; a shared import would break that boundary. `test_lenient_tool_call.py`/`test_step_interruption.py` (+18, VER-002 378 with only the 3 pre-existing `test_unmapped_*` failures, VER-001 52 OK, VER-003 12 OK) and the clean-room L2 red(12 failures on baseline)/green proof show sensitivity, not just greenness. No blocking architectural drift, boundary violation, or contract break found.\n\nVERDICT: ACCEPT","reason":null}}
